#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198950 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f86c71-5ba2-5262-9bbf-7f7163efa286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9({e}^{4}-e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493398} {:answer/id #uuid "3e6403cc-10a5-5873-b7f2-714dc7812c93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9({e}^{3}-e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493498} {:answer/id #uuid "c84e1d3d-5e71-590c-befa-918bc686cd64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5(1-{e}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493712} {:answer/id #uuid "d9cbdccf-0d8d-5a1e-bca5-5206ced248f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5({e}^{4}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493730} {:answer/id #uuid "f587b155-dbaf-5156-98a6-ca8c0ee16997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10({e}^{4}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493789}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493398} :answer-field/id #uuid "65ad75dc-fe53-5e66-9950-00b2a3fe62bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d1c41fe9-56fa-5a4c-8bab-8d810d33bac2" :question/math-academy-id "q-38371" :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=xy$ and $v=y+x,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}|y-x|\\,{e}^{xy}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le xy\\le 4,\\,1\\le y+x\\le 10\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=y+x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le 10\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =y-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n.\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{y-x}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}|y-x|\\,{e}^{xy}\\,dxdy & ={∬}_{Δ}|y-x|\\,{e}^{u}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|y-x|\\,{e}^{u}\\,\\frac{1}{|y-x|}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{1}^{10}{e}^{u}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{u}[{\\int }_{1}^{10}dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{u}[v]_{1}^{10}\\,du \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{1}^{4}{e}^{u}\\,du \\\\  & =9[{e}^{u}]_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =9({e}^{4}-e).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcc626f3-af02-5a96-b9d0-30aec8f61fe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375111} {:answer/id #uuid "00ed8726-d262-5be5-97e7-a1fe5ea12c43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375112} {:answer/id #uuid "a7036b0a-b817-5bc3-9b4e-f5631003b120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375113} {:answer/id #uuid "92615a0a-ce00-5262-b777-63a066308685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375114} {:answer/id #uuid "db58fbc6-bb95-536c-a184-99aae57b8126" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375115}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375113} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bce0f67-7ee4-58f3-b3ef-499ee3fa3477" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6722ea8b-a918-58be-8c65-d81640229453" :question/math-academy-id "q-38377" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{1+(x-y)^{2}}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 2\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{1+(x-y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,|-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,du]dv \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}du]dv \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}[u]_{0}^{2}\\,dv \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,dv \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡v]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198959 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04edc689-9e29-5c9b-8da2-da4420287986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{uv}3{w}^{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498740} {:answer/id #uuid "314e6482-a456-5bda-8904-887833458d85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}3\\sqrt{w}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498844} {:answer/id #uuid "57cc1828-6342-5346-b05d-7d143db10ddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{uv}3\\sqrt{w}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498931} {:answer/id #uuid "9156d344-2302-5c4e-b0e7-6f7c95dca531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}3{w}^{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499047} {:answer/id #uuid "cd799ece-9c4a-56a0-b053-cc390590f85c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{u+v}3w\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499183}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498740} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbe99af2-8c2c-533c-80a0-ca42ec737c5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "96fa59d6-c144-5393-a4a7-7b8198eb987c" :question/math-academy-id "q-38941" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le x+y\\le 2,\\,1\\le x-y\\le 2,\\,1\\le z\\le (x-y)(x+y)\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y,\\,w=\\sqrt{z},\n$$\n\nthe triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}3\\sqrt{z}\\,dV\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y,\\,w=\\sqrt{z}\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 2,\\,1\\le {w}^{2}\\le uv\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}(-1-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{w}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-w.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}3\\sqrt{z}\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}3w\\cdot |-w|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}3{w}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{uv}3{w}^{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "118c0a6d-2d86-5402-ab17-e9a5a70e791e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481820} {:answer/id #uuid "1a807e54-a4b6-5ff5-bbcc-a7cd1282fba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481845} {:answer/id #uuid "8da2e5ae-0945-59a5-8ed1-21ba6659c06f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482107} {:answer/id #uuid "97ba92a3-dcbc-53fc-b77a-c4ddb79e17c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482131} {:answer/id #uuid "feda8ec9-58b4-5dda-999b-6d6a16d95a6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482377}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482107} :answer-field/id #uuid "92bb2c37-88bf-542c-b72c-3da1ea32bcd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9d27a7ac-3737-5071-b997-af83c998bd7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-39054" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le 2x+y\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le y+z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-1\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,dV={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le u\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-1\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(1-0) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef7332e4-b981-54af-8da2-fcdb494e9902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197655} {:answer/id #uuid "281c6df6-036f-5860-be8c-11e9d432022a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197656} {:answer/id #uuid "7557b355-b5e6-5c35-9ec5-ce7baaeba82d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197658} {:answer/id #uuid "55d05532-6ff9-54f2-9d4c-bf3ea90714ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481979} {:answer/id #uuid "828f75f9-fca6-5a07-93ba-87dbb17ec87e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482075}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197655} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eb66677-2e9a-5652-8302-63d8739cb7ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3982f9b1-ca56-5e4b-8f97-4f23fb6f2fc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-39055" :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+y,\\,v=x+z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}(x+z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+y)\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le {x}^{2}+y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le x+z\\le 2,\\,-1\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+y,\\,v=x+z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,-1\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x+z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}v\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}dw \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡u]_{u=\\pi /6}^{u=\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{v=0}^{v=2}\\cdot [w]_{w=-1}^{w=1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-0)\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "881b0dd0-01c6-5013-9685-0e58c6da71c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197661} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a11a8a-6503-5229-93d1-630340faec00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197662} {:answer/id #uuid "33e5fb8a-a7fa-5959-b381-9183f9303802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197663} {:answer/id #uuid "3404d280-8cad-5644-8ec1-3e6e08824288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197664} {:answer/id #uuid "4cd7243b-48c8-50fa-aaa2-6aa1ef439e80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197665}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197663} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cd3b3e1-98ae-532b-94b3-c5b9b8ab641b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8b45928b-ff82-5faf-a3d8-f16998390abd" :question/math-academy-id "q-39060" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,1\\le \\sqrt{y}+x\\le 2,\\,1\\le \\frac{1}{z+1}\\le 5\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n1\\le y\\le 2,\\,1\\le \\sqrt{y}+x\\le 2,\\,1\\le \\frac{1}{z+1}\\le 5,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y,\\,v=\\sqrt{y}+x,\\,w=\\frac{1}{z+1}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 2,\\,1\\le w\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -(z+1)^{-2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -(z+1)^{-2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-(z+1)^{-2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(z+1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={w}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{{w}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{{w}^{2}}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{5}{w}^{-2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{5}{w}^{-2}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=1}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=1}^{v=2}\\cdot [-{w}^{-1}]_{w=1}^{w=5} \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199007 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bf91dd3-a758-5a4f-91d7-508b8452bd7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197917} {:answer/id #uuid "4cfe915e-292f-5afc-a987-feaf8645491b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480883} {:answer/id #uuid "50bbbf00-20cb-560c-a0ab-ee8b2c1c6005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480887} {:answer/id #uuid "69ed5ffb-e837-50a7-83fa-9b0d9378cfad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}-e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480936} {:answer/id #uuid "a5e7b49a-7376-5f77-b4cd-07e49c94ad93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e-2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481051}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480883} :answer-field/id #uuid "64ff9f93-9496-5f90-8fb6-64a192e2c4d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f7817742-b76a-5484-9fbf-d87d76ee15f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-82916" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+2y){e}^{(x+2y)^{2}}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 5,\\,0\\le x+2y\\le 1\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 5,\\,0\\le x+2y\\le 1,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x+2y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 5,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+2y){e}^{(x+2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}v{e}^{{v}^{2}}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}v{e}^{{v}^{2}}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{5}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}v{e}^{{v}^{2}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{5}{[\\frac{{e}^{{v}^{2}}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{5}(e-1)\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(e-1)[u]_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(e-1)(5-1) \\\\  & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233276 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bf6d318-67a3-589f-aa06-712a4c979552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512262} {:answer/id #uuid "5466b133-5ea5-525f-8cd7-1a549d321d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512288} {:answer/id #uuid "8a9a8734-2fa1-5cd5-97d6-c6ffcae6ba4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512426} {:answer/id #uuid "9b35183a-e748-54ed-8dd3-0bcbe740b891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512466} {:answer/id #uuid "ed844729-fa98-5650-835d-6732a8a4b167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512675}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512466} :answer-field/id #uuid "5749cf60-a3f1-5876-9c2b-03d5d66464f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "45f48cf6-e519-5407-abc6-4bbd59c8789a" :question/math-academy-id "q-119805" :question/problem "![](images/95/95c30eab094a24beb6976262167401efa973dbac12781c69d074eb14e12e2743.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $m+n?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $m+n.$ According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $m+n$ goes from the tail of $m$ to the head of $n:$ \n\n![](images/87/87647211f77776c5f5f740c205fafb150eef1b9d3ebefb82df73a9fdcd953bdb.png)\n\nIn $△AHB$, we have $∠AHB={90}^{∘}$, $AH=0.5=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $BH=2.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|m+n| & =AB \\\\  & =\\sqrt{A{H}^{2}+B{H}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $m+n$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}.$ Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{1}{|m+n|}\\cdot (m+n) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}(m+n).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0665e53-645a-508e-8a48-66627b805582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234874}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234874} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eb05908-2cad-5f19-9002-4431259f7c7c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1a131946-3c50-5cb9-8c20-5138d22bf7c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-167649" :question/problem "If we denote our surface of revolution by $S,$ then the total electric charge of the shell is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n\\displaystyle Q={∬}_{S}^{}d(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S=\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nPreviously, we determined that our surface can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [0,1],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nand the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖=2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(x,\\theta )$ into $d(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(r(x,\\theta )) & =\\frac{x({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x((2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{5}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}d(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}d(r(x,\\theta ))‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}}\\cdot 2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,8{x}^{7}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}8{x}^{7}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[{x}^{8}]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235513 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a057226-4563-56e2-8c25-e6d52f70db8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234756}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234756} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ebb2a22-14d9-5829-aa9d-41c29ede505f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3e60be6b-04aa-5311-85b6-7c960267e903" :question/math-academy-id "q-168035" :question/problem "Find the dot product of the vectors $q$ and $r.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$q\\cdot r=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The dot product of two vectors given in component form can be computed by summing up the products of corresponding components of vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q\\cdot r & ={q}_{x}\\cdot {r}_{x}+{q}_{y}\\cdot {r}_{y}+{q}_{z}\\cdot {r}_{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom part 1, we have $q=⟨0,-3,2⟩$ and $r=⟨0,4,3⟩.$ Therefore, the formula for the dot product gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q\\cdot r & =0\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =0-12+6 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235523 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce621436-9049-583a-b2ae-771c869c2cfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234871}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234871} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c365cba-2445-5f05-ab65-0272ed76fd58" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234999} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69e8d5d4-178f-5213-b5c2-28dce17a3e6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234707}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234707} :answer-field/id #uuid "b43a43c9-923a-541f-b025-831b9fdeb66b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bd4e7b23-65ca-5e51-94d4-c72686633d86" :question/math-academy-id "q-168114" :question/problem "Find the partial derivatives of $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V}$ at the point $(1,1).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{(1,\\hspace{0.167em}1)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{(1,\\hspace{0.167em}1)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V}) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+2x) \\\\  & =2y+2 \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V}) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+2x) \\\\  & =2x+0 \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{(1,\\hspace{0.167em}1)} & =2(1)+2 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V})\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{(1,\\hspace{0.167em}1)} & =2(1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235524 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7449510-b9e7-54a1-b3a0-5c5eb8a6f9da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234800}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234800} :answer-field/id #uuid "59a9ce7b-a143-5629-b507-bb3bccb07cce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235063} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae85fb59-c193-58ba-bf01-9ed467aac023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234813}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234813} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7ef65a3-d4ef-527d-bea7-f7bcb05085c1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8690a396-1b0d-570c-a12c-64188b2e8728" :question/math-academy-id "q-168115" :question/problem "Find the direction of the greatest rate of expansion of the fluid at the point $(1,1).$ Do not normalize the answer.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \nThe required direction is $∇(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=⟨$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "In part 2, we found that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient at $(1,1)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}\\,j \\\\  & =4\\,i+2\\,j \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235531 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7a3a887-6de4-535d-9d08-7728c412ff9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234859}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234859} :answer-field/id #uuid "48e9ca1b-260a-561e-bcfd-839e7bc6578a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c8217520-113c-5745-9a02-5051b4dbb017" :question/math-academy-id "q-168603" :question/problem "![](images/64/642a546fc498dfb0f01c0729959f9f68b8dc2d142d966673f204eda910f19211.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\nConsider the square $C$ with side length $8$ centered at $O,$ shown above. The flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{1}$ is given by\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}^{}\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}s.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{1}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $x$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=i.$\n\n![](images/19/1939a274191b89f69e51a929654489ed9038df0313f6e47b15e0ff55ad813066.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(({y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}{y}^{3}-1\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235540 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a292d0ec-0d72-578f-ac47-e9d6b19ac77c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2xy-2x-{y}^{2}+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234790}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234790} :answer-field/id #uuid "627ed6f9-5e55-5f96-a07b-04dde4b0b9fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6b5155bc-b492-5617-9be0-0783e4c496d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-168625" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the boundary of the top (tilted) surface of our solid. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above, as shown in the diagram. Use Stokes' theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation of the air along $C.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \nThe required circulation is $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}^{}F\\cdot dr\\,={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \n$\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our top (tilted) surface ${S}_{T}$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=4-x-y.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-x-y)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨2xy,\\,2z-{y}^{2},\\,2y⟩{|}_{z=4-x-y} \\\\  & =⟨2xy,\\,2(4-x-y)-{y}^{2},\\,2y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{2xy}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{8-2x-2y-{y}^{2}}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{2y}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{T}}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(2xy)\\cdot (-1)-(8-2x-2y-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-1)+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy-2x-{y}^{2}+8)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/d0/d0f9cb64d0ae1c3615df5a05c9f580bb9c74f6529efd732cfd83e1f4328ff8a3.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(2xy-2x-{y}^{2}+8)\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2xy-2x-{y}^{2}+8\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235542 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bcf71f90-cbb8-5937-bf8c-aa426f84fa71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4{y}^{3}-10{x}^{2}y+20y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234835}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234835} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe580988-3de6-51d3-ad7e-b78106efb226" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0004c300-7b2f-5627-8c54-a9a44f3c9175" :question/math-academy-id "q-168862" :question/problem "$\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{y}^{4}-5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+10{y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}+2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{4})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{4})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the constant multiple rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{4})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10{y}^{2}) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{4})-5{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})+10\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4{y}^{3}-5{x}^{2}\\cdot 2y+10\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =-4{y}^{3}-10{x}^{2}y+20y\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237826 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00754d46-0704-5773-b914-479b2780c54e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235797} {:answer/id #uuid "0b160a2e-ac6a-5783-b728-e3786b2e8ea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235873} {:answer/id #uuid "5e18d896-af8f-5c4e-a92e-a5e7a5d02427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236408} {:answer/id #uuid "aa42023a-fee9-57d2-8160-bb0856641f36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236893} {:answer/id #uuid "fd10e16a-4040-5c60-bbca-1fcbbc9e602c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237421}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235873} :answer-field/id #uuid "2955f949-c61e-5c52-a7ce-dd2c578781b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "424bd239-6062-5739-8f7b-4979cfdd4107" :question/math-academy-id "q-101406" :question/problem "Let $p=⟨1,-2⟩$ and $q=⟨4,-1⟩.$ Find $p\\cdot q.$" :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4+(-2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237827 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35170554-7491-5b34-9b67-ab6efae808e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236152} {:answer/id #uuid "49bebe95-7d8f-5363-8791-a41d117d2f9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236287} {:answer/id #uuid "77b5df1a-8495-5220-8d4a-3d2d72b7ceae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236568} {:answer/id #uuid "84346316-be9d-55ce-ad43-19e661050d2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236640} {:answer/id #uuid "9da633c8-52af-507c-8a35-a65c81ee25b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236802}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236568} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f7b6300-7056-5f11-ba41-499cc6516307" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dfd0ffab-79f2-5643-9530-8f55bce579c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-101417" :question/problem "Let $u=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 2x\\end{bmatrix}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ x\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that $u⊥v$ and $x<0$, find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $u⊥v$ ($u$ and $v$ are perpendicular) if and only if $u\\cdot v=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{x}\\cdot {v}_{x}+{u}_{y}\\cdot {v}_{y} \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-2)+2x\\cdot x \\\\  & =-18+2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ -18+2{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9 \\\\ x & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the positive solution since we are told that $x<0.$ Therefore, $x=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237842 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ae7ead7-181b-5833-a116-31f0377cd8b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236084} {:answer/id #uuid "70b2c173-e023-59b4-bf4f-792730e93db7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236532} {:answer/id #uuid "70c2cf83-482d-5dae-9db6-502db0b85ef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236533} {:answer/id #uuid "84dcc40c-1ac0-5618-9182-fd3c12380aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236642} {:answer/id #uuid "a8a8596c-db12-5375-bbc6-35c46196b6ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236874}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236533} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc8d9514-9eda-561e-b725-77622b71dc92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "da039a17-2a9d-5f24-9d07-024eac6133bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-101522" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,a\\,|=14$ and the angle between vectors $a$ and $b$ is $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =14\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =14\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237843 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38bbd13f-4e19-56c7-97cf-a7f2930fd2a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236177} {:answer/id #uuid "6ae97b09-85cc-5483-a73f-2156503e44f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236491} {:answer/id #uuid "acec17c2-c6d1-51d8-9717-1aad0ba22bd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236919} {:answer/id #uuid "dc65c71f-98e3-5c9e-8c5a-5f34a94dc985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237207} {:answer/id #uuid "dd7f754a-d29e-5a57-9e04-ca3652a8b431" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237211}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236177} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0696449-028c-5725-a83a-78022f2c13a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b928033f-deef-5783-b89b-20e39d738b30" :question/math-academy-id "q-101523" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,p\\,|=10\\sqrt{2}$ and the angle between vectors $p$ and $q$ is $\\theta ={45}^{∘}.$ Find the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p & =|\\,p\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =10\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237844 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cbc5af6-d204-54d2-ade2-1a5cecbf2a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236199} {:answer/id #uuid "443bbd68-982d-51d1-a030-c0f50796f888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236257} {:answer/id #uuid "49406efc-d1f8-5161-b4db-370aec598a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236285} {:answer/id #uuid "76bb2467-e419-5c6d-99e9-415ecc0eb0a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236562} {:answer/id #uuid "a9fc4155-d22f-5aae-b5c8-3b51cb959c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236889}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236257} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6f49c7d-7ef0-518b-a6e1-9fc9dcaec38c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c9e0b18d-47ac-57ac-a417-f1e364d929df" :question/math-academy-id "q-101526" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨3,-1,-1⟩$ and $b=⟨4,4,2⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00fb3c3e-d9e3-5ca7-9ef7-0ed02505d36d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235803} {:answer/id #uuid "0ca2b49c-358f-5298-bb0c-b08f2cee1347" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235883} {:answer/id #uuid "380b0194-c4b4-56b5-9971-32c96bf760cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236173} {:answer/id #uuid "9f9c9d40-b950-5d9b-8f85-cb17c877f300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236812} {:answer/id #uuid "e7abb70a-d7a1-5710-9223-38dfeb03d6b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237273}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237273} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d409f4f-042c-52fb-aeee-290ca5cc15f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4a8ce835-c1ae-59af-9549-b0aa4dd68f54" :question/math-academy-id "q-101527" :question/problem "Let $p=⟨3,2,1⟩$ and $q=⟨1,-1,1⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p & =\\frac{p\\cdot q}{|\\,q\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237846 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fa40eb2-c180-538d-b522-de9d9f83d889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236019} {:answer/id #uuid "695ae008-d47c-57f9-9587-05c4c533f86b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236484} {:answer/id #uuid "a24abdba-ec13-587e-b963-32e6b30a415c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236834} {:answer/id #uuid "aef8d084-d169-5e09-9f0e-1801f381f55c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236932} {:answer/id #uuid "e2447701-f8a9-51c7-9d3a-46b568b874f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237235}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236484} :answer-field/id #uuid "edfc2aca-9792-5f3e-b0d4-4cac4d28ecd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "144f5038-f97a-55ee-aff4-8a719828b869" :question/math-academy-id "q-101528" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨2,-1,2⟩$ and $b=⟨6,2,-3⟩.$ Find ${\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a-{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 6+(-1)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-3)}{\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b & =\\frac{b\\cdot a}{|\\,a\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a-{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b & =\\frac{4}{7}-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{7}-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{3}{21}-\\frac{7}{21}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237847 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65481007-8af1-5f1c-8985-c322b0b2eaad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236460} {:answer/id #uuid "8e0e3d4f-455b-56e0-8e43-c37201bd7145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236717} {:answer/id #uuid "c92389fa-897e-5ad9-8bf2-97d05c95f4cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237091} {:answer/id #uuid "cec3e8ae-9080-53fc-b8e8-bceb2f549aeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237128} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f4c0e9-5bc2-5b74-a144-0c1547768fc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237397}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237091} :answer-field/id #uuid "44dddb26-f4fb-57da-99dc-4c1ff9c89ba9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237526}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d1b1c513-b8bc-52c7-b4e0-fc1349d07866" :question/math-academy-id "q-101529" :question/problem "Let $p=⟨1,\\sqrt{6},3⟩$ and $q=⟨-7,\\sqrt{6},3⟩.$ Find ${\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p+{\\text{comp}}_{p}\\,q.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p & =\\frac{p\\cdot q}{|\\,q\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-7)+\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}+3\\cdot 3}{\\sqrt{(-7)^{2}+{\\sqrt{6}}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{64}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{p}\\,q & =\\frac{p\\cdot q}{|\\,p\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{p}_{x}^{2}+{p}_{y}^{2}+{p}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{\\sqrt{6}}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p+{\\text{comp}}_{p}\\,q & =1+2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1adb26c2-291f-5ecb-be27-a83497de5b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238279} {:answer/id #uuid "a4933581-9ab8-5174-ab43-a4a7af154ea9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239482} {:answer/id #uuid "add0b6a3-0052-52a8-a4b1-57278ac77df3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239563} {:answer/id #uuid "b0815d5d-2eab-5eb6-b0b6-cea74e67c474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239588} {:answer/id #uuid "bc186f6a-3c28-5f17-b90f-9614a603b361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239695}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239482} :answer-field/id #uuid "46f03b38-7ac7-5b2a-bebf-d97ba46b022c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7b025c1d-a393-5ea5-8c4e-6e0786f6f25d" :question/math-academy-id "q-104617" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=2x$ for  $x\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2\\pi )-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =(0+1)-(0+1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a528a36-e97e-5732-8fae-1697a81d8ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238153} {:answer/id #uuid "44bbd131-4ecd-540b-ad14-259cefa4dcf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238651} {:answer/id #uuid "4ab4b784-0b5f-5e1b-9fde-ec17a7e2873d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238701} {:answer/id #uuid "89f290a7-499e-5b23-8f65-ac96513f25dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239255} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb83a5e-cf58-56d7-bc9a-928b68a83802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240135}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240135} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbad1630-befd-5b0d-9389-d98b46343814" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1eb7495c-4bb1-5f06-bc1d-e458481de613" :question/math-academy-id "q-104623" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y-{y}^{2}x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}\\cdot 2-{2}^{2}\\cdot \\pi )-({0}^{2}\\cdot 0-{0}^{2}\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =(2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =2{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240907 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "324fc058-3716-52e2-915c-a8ff8548ce66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238492} {:answer/id #uuid "40f10544-0cd1-50c6-9897-c035b59965c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238616} {:answer/id #uuid "99009bfa-183e-50ad-b31d-4a8cd8b1b4fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239384} {:answer/id #uuid "ae9e232f-a132-5ae1-a985-ee43b1e859ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e-{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239575} {:answer/id #uuid "b4578f1a-d647-5132-b9c6-b097afd1fc07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239632}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239632} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bc00398-8a1c-5911-a2b1-71601fb149ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "45e3e133-a99c-58ca-b74f-4cbfe235bb72" :question/math-academy-id "q-104625" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}\\right).$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y={x}^{3}+1$ for $x\\in [1,2],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{3}+1⟩,\\,t\\in [1,2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨1,2⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2,9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,2)$ and $(2,9).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f(2,9)-f(1,2) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{9\\pi }{2}\\right)-{e}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot 1-{e}^{1}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240908 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cb86dc4-a7cd-5ca1-b598-954b892dcccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "171" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238168} {:answer/id #uuid "23431fc6-f04f-5360-af6d-30876ddc94bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "146" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238365} {:answer/id #uuid "8bcde37b-9a32-506b-bf91-9dcabb76716e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "198" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239270} {:answer/id #uuid "9b44e22a-7576-5afa-b3b8-4a77db65eb5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239409} {:answer/id #uuid "a90ce1ba-c1cf-56f7-b6bb-6a5a1b7fdca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "213" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239532}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238168} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ae2dfe3-e747-5345-997b-435580cc53ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "893763e7-56bb-5bf7-9f2b-99426982714b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104626" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}).$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [-1,2],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in [-1,2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨-1,2⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(-1,2)$ and $(2,8).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(-1)) \\\\  & =f(2,8)-f(-1,2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({2}^{3}+{8}^{3})-\\frac{1}{3}((-1)^{3}+{2}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(520)-\\frac{1}{3}(7) \\\\  & =\\frac{520}{3}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =171.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240909 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e699747-9682-50ad-bc3c-89217394ac15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238310} {:answer/id #uuid "2607fd46-ddb8-5e68-96ad-e64aed38f812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238385} {:answer/id #uuid "897eb260-f9ce-5892-bb70-7962baeb2fe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239250} {:answer/id #uuid "95fcd466-856f-5f24-95dd-68c6e3fb2527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239353} {:answer/id #uuid "e5429a9a-ce6f-502f-af4d-a0421c2ce2a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240070}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239353} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c7cb5bc-2de6-517b-a738-225b68f34e76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "377033ea-e89e-5b49-a8b3-206ce2c51bb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-104640" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(2\\pi ) & =⟨1,0,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0,0)$ and $(1,0,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,0,2\\pi )-f(1,0,0) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}\\cdot 2\\pi +1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0)-({1}^{2}\\cdot 0+1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =(2\\pi +0)-0 \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240918 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dc17ac8-c43f-5795-99c6-96724b983951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+{e}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238304} {:answer/id #uuid "8ebf9cef-e23a-5e13-9dc3-bf7b4436f362" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239296} {:answer/id #uuid "cee04a38-9ba8-5587-a832-c024ade625b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{3}+{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239868} {:answer/id #uuid "fee0c938-94f4-5d82-a936-a0e6d4c287bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{3}-2{e}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240311} {:answer/id #uuid "ffbb53c3-5822-5141-810e-1e1ceaf85e79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240326}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240311} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe8d7464-8b34-59c0-a3ab-f6d1c3d9d9bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "227ca00b-2249-5c8a-8773-a9971e9ef701" :question/math-academy-id "q-104725" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{e}^{t}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for $F=(3{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2x)\\,i+(3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4y)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =3{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,e⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(1,e).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,e)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =({1}^{3}\\cdot {e}^{3}+{1}^{2}-2\\cdot {e}^{2})-({0}^{3}\\cdot {1}^{3}+{0}^{2}-2\\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{3}+1-2{e}^{2}+2 \\\\  & ={e}^{3}-2{e}^{2}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240919 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "741bc569-8dcc-54a7-93f1-c3d9b892adc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239049} {:answer/id #uuid "76597f8f-a721-552f-bc46-44e414558f67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e({e}^{4{\\pi }^{2}}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239077} {:answer/id #uuid "a05094d0-34c2-5de5-a965-8a05daab4caa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{4\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239454} {:answer/id #uuid "a811fdf6-0ca8-5ba9-9b33-e28fa7b6d1c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239513} {:answer/id #uuid "dbca776b-42f8-568c-8d08-0e204fad75f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e({e}^{2\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239973}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239077} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c402c1d-d0dd-5148-a3d3-ef8f8ca1b754" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "30f2f02c-3a13-5617-8d20-05ffc71d40c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-104728" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+1,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for $F={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r(2\\pi ) & =⟨4{\\pi }^{2}+1,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0)$ and $(4{\\pi }^{2}+1,2\\pi ).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(4{\\pi }^{2}+1,2\\pi )-f(1,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{4{\\pi }^{2}+1}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi -{e}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & ={e}^{4{\\pi }^{2}+1}-e \\\\  & =e({e}^{4{\\pi }^{2}}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
